---
/* Ported from docs/kitchen-sink/typing-effect.html (CCWEB2-317 phase 3).
 *
 * Shell extraction. Mounting pattern and why it is not an island: button.astro.

 * The only page whose specimen needs the gsap and gsap/dist/SplitText entries
 * in the import map. They are in it on all thirteen because the map is one
 * shared prop; this is the page that proves they resolve. */
import DocPage from '../../layouts/DocPage.astro';
/* Imported, not linked: Vite has to COMPILE this one. A raw href would be
   copied through as-is with its @import "tailwindcss/..." unresolved. */
import '../../tailwind.css';
---
<DocPage
  title="TypingEffect — CODECAVE storybook"
  nav="kitchen-sink"
  stylesheets={['storybook.css']}
>
<div class="sb">
  <a class="sb-back" href="index.html">&larr; All components</a>

  <header class="sb-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Content · 11 of 13</p>
    <h1>TypingEffect</h1>
    <p>The two-line display heading. Its second line is one of the few sanctioned
       uses of <code>#5F20FE</code> on text — legitimate only because it runs at
       32–56px, where the 3:1 large-text floor applies.</p>
    <div class="sb-tags">
      <span class="sb-tag is-vue">Vue island</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">src/components/common/effects/TypingEffect.vue</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">rendered live from src/components/common/effects/TypingEffect.vue</span>
      <span class="sb-tag">GSAP SplitText</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Props</h2>
    <table class="sb-props">
      <thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>Type</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>text1 <span class="sb-req">*</span></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>First line, <code>--color-heading</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>text2 <span class="sb-req">*</span></td><td><code>string</code></td><td>Second line, <code>--color-action</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Stories</h2>
    <p>Live — this is the real component and the real GSAP SplitText (3.15,
       vendored). On mount, once <code>document.fonts.ready</code> resolves, it
       breaks the text into characters and staggers them from
       <code>opacity: 0.5</code> at 0.1s intervals. Reload to replay.</p>
    <div class="sb-stories is-single">

      <div class="sb-story">
        <div class="sb-canvas is-tall">
          <div id="story-typing"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="sb-story-body">
          <span class="sb-story-name">Default</span>
          <span class="sb-story-args">text1="We build software" text2="that survives…"</span>
          <span class="sb-story-note">2rem base, stepping to 3.5rem at the lg breakpoint. Bold, 130% leading.</span>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sb-section">
    <h2>Findings</h2>

    <p class="sb-note is-warn"><strong>The selector is global, so multiple instances collide.</strong>
      <code>SplitText.create('.split-text span', …)</code> queries the whole
      document, not the component root. A second TypingEffect on the same page
      causes each mount to re-split <em>every</em> instance's spans — including
      ones already split — and the later mount animates characters belonging to
      the earlier one. The fix is a template ref scoped to this component.</p>

    <p class="sb-note is-warn"><strong>No reduced-motion guard.</strong>
      The stagger runs unconditionally from <code>onMounted</code> and again on
      every prop change. It is GSAP rather than CSS, and the current site ships
      no <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> handling at all — this is the
      site's largest piece of moving text, so it is the animation most worth
      guarding.</p>

    <p class="sb-note is-warn"><strong><code>gsap.registerPlugin</code> runs on every call.</strong>
      It sits inside <code>splitAnimation</code>, so it re-registers on mount and
      on each <code>watch</code> firing rather than once at module scope. Harmless
      but repeated, and it makes the watch path heavier than it needs to be.</p>

    <p class="sb-note"><strong>Waiting on <code>document.fonts.ready</code> is the right call.</strong>
      Splitting before Satoshi loads would measure fallback glyphs and produce
      character boxes that jump when the real font arrives. The component defers
      until fonts settle — worth preserving in any rewrite.</p>

    <p class="sb-note"><strong>The heading is an <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> with two block spans.</strong>
      Both lines live inside one heading element, so the accessible name is the
      full sentence — correct. The <code>:key="text1"</code> forces a fresh node
      when the copy changes, which is what makes the <code>watch</code> re-split
      work.</p>
  </section>

</div>

<script type="module">
  import { createApp } from 'vue';
  import TypingEffect from '../../../src/components/common/effects/TypingEffect.vue';

  createApp(TypingEffect, {
    text1: 'We build software',
    text2: 'that survives contact with your business.',
  }).mount('#story-typing');
</script>
</DocPage>
